  <p>
    We're so accustomed to gravity that we rarely notice it, yet it profoundly affects nearly everything we do.
    From the moment you wake up to when you fall asleep, gravity shapes your experience in countless subtle and
    not-so-subtle ways.
  </p>

  <h2>Your Body and Gravity</h2>

  <h3>Blood Circulation</h3>
  <p>
    Your cardiovascular system evolved to pump blood upward against gravity. When you stand up quickly, gravity
    temporarily pools blood in your legs, sometimes causing dizziness until your body compensates. This is why
    astronauts face cardiovascular challenges—their bodies decondition when not working against gravity.
  </p>

  <h3>Posture and Muscles</h3>
  <p>
    Standing upright requires constant muscle activation to counteract gravity. Your skeletal muscles, particularly
    in your legs and back, are continuously working just to keep you vertical. This constant work maintains muscle
    tone and bone density.
  </p>

  <h3>Aging and Height</h3>
  <p>
    Gravity compresses your spine throughout the day. You're actually about 1-2 centimeters taller in the morning
    than evening as spinal discs decompress during sleep. Over decades, gravity's cumulative effects contribute to
    the height loss associated with aging.
  </p>

  <h2>Cooking and Food</h2>

  <h3>Convection and Mixing</h3>
  <p>
    Gravity drives convection in cooking. Hot liquid rises while cool liquid sinks, creating mixing currents that
    distribute heat. Without gravity, cooking would work completely differently—astronauts can't have open flames
    because hot gases don't rise in microgravity.
  </p>

  <h3>Bubbles and Fizz</h3>
  <p>
    Carbonated beverages rely on gravity to make bubbles rise. In space, bubbles don't separate from liquid,
    making carbonated drinks undrinkable (they'd create painful gas in the stomach). Even brewing coffee requires
    gravity to separate grounds from liquid.
  </p>

  <h2>Construction and Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Every building is designed to resist gravity's pull. Structural engineers must account for gravitational loads—
    the weight of materials and contents. Gravity determines how tall we can build, what materials we need, and
    how structures must be reinforced.
  </p>

  <h3>The Level and Plumb</h3>
  <p>
    Construction tools like levels and plumb bobs use gravity as a reference. "Level" means perpendicular to
    gravity's direction, while "plumb" means parallel to it. These definitions come directly from gravity creating
    a universal reference direction.
  </p>

  <h2>Sports and Athletics</h2>

  <h3>Trajectory and Timing</h3>
  <p>
    Every sport involving thrown or hit objects relies on understanding ballistic trajectories shaped by gravity.
    Basketball players instinctively calculate parabolic arcs. Baseball players judge fly balls by understanding
    how gravity affects motion.
  </p>

  <h3>High Jump and Long Jump</h3>
  <p>
    Olympic records in jumping events are fundamentally limited by gravity. On the Moon, with one-sixth Earth's
    gravity, an Olympic high jumper could clear about 12 meters. On Jupiter, they'd struggle to jump at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Technology and Precision</h2>

  <h3>GPS Satellites</h3>
  <p>
    Your smartphone's GPS must account for relativistic time dilation caused by gravity. Satellite clocks run
    faster than Earth-based clocks due to weaker gravitational field. Without these corrections, GPS would accumulate
    errors of about 10 kilometers per day.
  </p>

  <h3>Precision Instruments</h3>
  <p>
    Ultra-precise devices like atomic clocks and quantum computers must account for local gravity variations.
    Moving a clock vertically by one meter changes its tick rate measurably. This precision enables new applications
    in geodesy and fundamental physics research.
  </p>

  <h2>Water and Plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    Water towers and plumbing systems use gravity to create water pressure. The height difference between water
    storage and your faucet determines pressure. This is why buildings have water tanks on roofs and why water
    pressure is higher on lower floors.
  </p>

  <h3>Drainage</h3>
  <p>
    All drainage systems rely on gravity. Sinks, toilets, and street drains use gravity to move water without
    pumps. Plumbers must carefully calculate slopes in pipes to ensure proper drainage—too shallow won't flow,
    too steep creates problems with air locks.
  </p>

  <h2>Sleep and Rest</h2>
  <p>
    Sleep position affects how gravity influences blood flow and organ position. Sleeping with your head elevated
    can reduce snoring and acid reflux by using gravity to your advantage. Back pain often improves when gravity's
    compressive effects are reduced during horizontal rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Gravity as Reference</h2>
  <p>
    Gravity provides a universal "down" direction that we use to orient ourselves and objects. Indoor environments,
    furniture, buildings—everything is designed relative to gravity's direction. This reference is so fundamental
    we only notice it when absent, like in space or virtual reality.
  </p>

  <h3>Inner Ear and Balance</h3>
  <p>
    Your vestibular system (inner ear) uses gravity to sense orientation and maintain balance. Tiny crystals in
    fluid-filled chambers shift with gravity, sending signals about your head position. This system can malfunction
    in microgravity, causing space motion sickness.
  </p>

